  ],
  "text": "A tea stall and a luxury showroom now take payment the same way, and neither one touches cash. For almost all of history, paying meant physically handing something over. Then cards, which were faster but needed a machine, a fee, and a bank on each side. Plenty of the world skipped that step entirely. India went from cash straight to instant transfer, with a printed square as the whole terminal. Billions of transfers a month, most of them too small for a card fee to survive. That is the real change. When a payment costs almost nothing, tiny payments become possible. A five rupee transfer is not worth a card. It is worth a scan. The trade is that every one is recorded, and none of them work when the network is down. Money stopped being a thing you carry. It became a message you send. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
